{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"occlusion handling occlusion handling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md","fileName":"Occlusion Handling.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Occlusion Handling","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Augmented Reality Visual Coherence]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Augmented Reality Visual Coherence","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"occlusion handling occlusion handling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md","fileName":"Occlusion Handling.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Occlusion Handling","heading":"Occlusion Handling","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Occlusion handling determines which real or virtual surface should be visible at each image location. A real object in front of a virtual object must hide it, while a virtual object nearer to the viewpoint must cover the corresponding real pixels.\n\nCorrect depth ordering requires geometry or image-derived evidence about the physical scene. Missing or inaccurate boundaries create conspicuous cutouts because occlusion is a strong depth cue.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Augmented Reality Visual Coherence","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"occlusion handling occlusion handling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Occlusion Handling.m